description:
User's request: "can we create a tool that takes the underlying meshnode graph of a dsl and shows its run time in the engine using boxes and bezier arrows connecting the different parts of the dsl". It is to be drawn inside Ixdar, not as a web page: "pan zoom already exist in the 2d engine, bezier shader already exists, need rounded rect sdf, reuse circles, dashed lines and hyperstrings".

Why: a DSL such as a crawfish model runs dozens of nodes, and today the only view of where the time goes is the `[dsl-timing]` log line from MeshNodeViewerScene.logTiming and the `mesh dsl timing` route (platform/automation/endpoints/mesh/dsl/Timing.java), a flat list. The graph's shape (which node feeds which, where the slow chain is) is not visible anywhere.

What exists: NodeGraphRuntime.executeGraphResult already times every statement (lastTimingMs and lastPeakHeapBytes, keyed "id (type)", plus lastTotalMs), and the parsed statements (PythonParser.ParsedNode with NodeReference arguments) already give the edges: an argument that is a NodeReference is an arrow from ref.nodeId's port ref.portName to this node's port. Function calls (PythonParser.FunctionDef) are timed as one statement. The 2D engine has Camera2D pan and zoom, SDFBezier (glsl/sdf_bezier.fs), SDFCircle, SDFLine (dashed), and HyperString text.

Wanted: a 2D scene (or a mode of the mesh viewer scene) that runs or reads the last run of a DSL and draws:
- one rounded-rectangle box per statement, labelled with its id and node type in HyperString, and its run time in ms (and peak heap) inside the box;
- the box tinted by its share of the total run time, so the slow nodes stand out (a Palette from IX-25, not new literals);
- input and output ports as small circles on the box edges, named;
- a bezier arrow from each output port to every input that references it;
- a layered left-to-right layout by dependency depth, with crossings reduced;
- pan and zoom through the existing Camera2D;
- a status line with the total run time and the slowest chain (critical path) through the graph.

A rounded-rectangle SDF is the one new primitive: add it next to SDFCircle/SDFLine in graphics/render/sdf, sharing their shader setup.
